Solution: Since no project code was provided, I will use a minimal, self-contained example that demonstrates the symptom **Wrong Container Behavior** caused by **Wrong Code Logic**.

## Overview

The container is intended to be a **bounded FIFO queue**:

- `add(item)` places the item at the back of the queue.
- `remove()` removes the item from the front of the queue.

The buggy implementation uses the `tail` pointer in `remove()` instead of the `head` pointer. This makes the container behave like a **LIFO stack**, returning the most recently added item first instead of the oldest item first.

---

## Buggy Container Class

```java
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class Container<T> {

    private final Object[] elements;
    private int head;   // index of the oldest element
    private int tail;   // index where the next element will be inserted
    private int size;

    public Container(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Capacity must be positive");
        }
        elements = new Object[capacity];
    }

    // Inserts an item at the back of the container
    public void add(T item) {
        if (size == elements.length) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Container is full");
        }
        elements[tail] = item;
        tail = (tail + 1) % elements.length;
        size++;
    }

    // Removes an item from the container
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T remove() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Container is empty");
        }

        // BUG: Reads from the tail instead of the head.
        // This makes the container return the newest element, not the oldest.
        T item = (T) elements[(tail - 1 + elements.length) % elements.length];

        // BUG: Also moves the tail backward instead of moving the head forward.
        tail = (tail - 1 + elements.length) % elements.length;

        size--;
        return item;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == elements.length;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
}
```

---

## Expected Behavior

For a FIFO queue:

```text
Container capacity = 3

add("first")
add("second")
add("third")

remove() -> "first"
remove() -> "second"
remove() -> "third"
```

The container must preserve insertion order.

---

## Actual Incorrect Behavior

With the buggy `remove()`:

```text
Container capacity = 3

add("first")
add("second")
add("third")

remove() -> "third"   // incorrect
remove() -> "second"  // incorrect
remove() -> "first"   // incorrect
```

The container behaves like a stack:

```text
remove() -> "third"
remove() -> "second"
remove() -> "first"
```

This is the visible symptom: **wrong container behavior**.

---

## Test Case That Reveals the Discrepancy

JUnit test:

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

public class ContainerTest {

    @Test
    public void removeShouldReturnElementsInInsertionOrder() {
        Container<String> container = new Container<>(3);

        container.add("first");
        container.add("second");
        container.add("third");

        assertEquals("first", container.remove());
        assertEquals("second", container.remove());
        assertEquals("third", container.remove());
    }
}
```

Failure output from JUnit:

```text
expected: <first> but was: <third>
```

Or depending on the test framework:

```text
Expected: first
Actual:   third
```

No exception is thrown because the modulo arithmetic keeps the array index inside bounds. The failure is purely logical.

---

## Root Cause

The root cause is in the `remove()` method:

```java
T item = (T) elements[(tail - 1 + elements.length) % elements.length];
tail = (tail - 1 + elements.length) % elements.length;
```

The method should use `head`, not `tail`:

- `head` points to the oldest element.
- `tail` points to the next free slot for insertion.
- `remove()` should read from `head`, then advance `head`.
- `add()` writes to `tail`, then advances `tail`.

The buggy code reads from the most recently inserted position and then moves the insertion pointer backward. The `head` field is never used during removal, so the queue logic is silently replaced with stack logic.

---

## Corrected Implementation

Only the `remove()` method changes:

```java
@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
public T remove() {
    if (size == 0) {
        throw new NoSuchElementException("Container is empty");
    }

    // Correct: read from the oldest element
    T item = (T) elements[head];

    // Correct: advance the head pointer
    head = (head + 1) % elements.length;

    size--;
    return item;
}
```

Full corrected class:

```java
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class Container<T> {

    private final Object[] elements;
    private int head;
    private int tail;
    private int size;

    public Container(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Capacity must be positive");
        }
        elements = new Object[capacity];
    }

    public void add(T item) {
        if (size == elements.length) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Container is full");
        }
        elements[tail] = item;
        tail = (tail + 1) % elements.length;
        size++;
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T remove() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Container is empty");
        }
        T item = (T) elements[head];
        head = (head + 1) % elements.length;
        size--;
        return item;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == elements.length;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
}
```

---

## Additional Diagnostic Example

A simple manual check:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Container<String> container = new Container<>(2);

    container.add("A");
    container.add("B");

    String firstRemoved = container.remove();

    System.out.println("First removed: " + firstRemoved);

    if (!"A".equals(firstRemoved)) {
        System.out.println("BUG REPRODUCED: Expected A, got " + firstRemoved);
    }
}
```

Buggy output:

```text
First removed: B
BUG REPRODUCED: Expected A, got B
```

Correct output:

```text
First removed: A
```

---

## Summary

| Aspect                    | Buggy Version                   | Correct Version               |
|---------------------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------|
| Container behavior        | LIFO / stack                    | FIFO / queue                  |
| `remove()` reads from     | `tail` (newest item)            | `head` (oldest item)          |
| After removal             | `tail` is decremented           | `head` is incremented         |
| Test result               | `expected: <first> but was: <third>` | All assertions pass      |
| Root cause type           | Wrong code logic                | Fixed logic                   |

The wrong container behavior is a direct result of the logical error in `remove()`: using the insertion pointer (`tail`) to select the element to remove, instead of the removal pointer (`head`). Correcting that one line restores the intended FIFO container behavior.

